WEBVTT

00:00.210 --> 00:02.670
Instructor: So now let's try a fast forward merge,

00:02.670 --> 00:05.430
which just to recap from two minutes ago

00:05.430 --> 00:06.870
at the end of the last video,

00:06.870 --> 00:09.390
a fast forward merge is a special case of a merge.

00:09.390 --> 00:12.270
It's a simpler merge to perform.

00:12.270 --> 00:13.560
The commands are the same,

00:13.560 --> 00:16.890
but from Gits perspective it's really easy

00:16.890 --> 00:19.590
because all it has to do is move a pointer up

00:19.590 --> 00:21.660
some number of commits to catch up

00:21.660 --> 00:25.620
or to fast forward to some particular destination commit.

00:25.620 --> 00:26.880
So in these diagrams,

00:26.880 --> 00:29.280
we're fast forwarding Master to commits

00:29.280 --> 00:32.670
if we merge the Bugfix branch into Master.

00:32.670 --> 00:33.840
So that's what we're gonna practice.

00:33.840 --> 00:35.010
We're gonna try this out.

00:35.010 --> 00:37.800
Remember that this is a special case of a merge.

00:37.800 --> 00:39.300
Not all merges work this way.

00:39.300 --> 00:41.520
And also remember the steps.

00:41.520 --> 00:43.590
The first step is to switch

00:43.590 --> 00:46.770
to the destination branch we're merging into.

00:46.770 --> 00:49.290
So back here, if we go with these diagrams,

00:49.290 --> 00:51.330
we need to switch over to Master

00:51.330 --> 00:53.490
if we weren't on Master already ,

00:53.490 --> 00:56.730
assuming we want to merge these commits into Master.

00:56.730 --> 00:59.550
Then all we do is run git merge

00:59.550 --> 01:02.970
and then some branch we want to merge into Master.

01:02.970 --> 01:06.330
And then in this case, that will make Master catch up

01:06.330 --> 01:08.160
on those commits from Bugfix.

01:08.160 --> 01:09.630
In other situations,

01:09.630 --> 01:12.090
we might have some additional work to do.

01:12.090 --> 01:13.890
In the event that there's additional commits

01:13.890 --> 01:16.260
on Master that don't exist on Bugfix

01:16.260 --> 01:18.090
or there's conflicting information,

01:18.090 --> 01:21.060
that is what we'll talk about in the next couple of videos.

01:21.060 --> 01:22.230
But for now, we're just gonna practice

01:22.230 --> 01:23.670
with a fast forward merge.

01:23.670 --> 01:24.970
So switch to the branch

01:25.847 --> 01:27.270
and then merge some other branch in.

01:27.270 --> 01:28.920
Okay, so let's try it out.

01:28.920 --> 01:31.890
-: So over here on our playlist example,

01:31.890 --> 01:35.010
we have, well, quite a few branches.

01:35.010 --> 01:37.260
I haven't shown this, but if we use dash v

01:37.260 --> 01:39.030
we get a little bit more information.

01:39.030 --> 01:42.300
We get the last commits, the tip of the branch,

01:42.300 --> 01:46.020
and the commit message alongside the branch name.

01:46.020 --> 01:48.600
So we started off with our Master branch.

01:48.600 --> 01:49.920
It's very simple, right?

01:49.920 --> 01:54.180
If I switch to it, it just has these two Abba songs.

01:54.180 --> 01:58.170
We look at git log, we have two commits there.

01:58.170 --> 01:59.730
And then we have our Oldies branch

01:59.730 --> 02:01.890
which I think I'm gonna merge into Master.

02:01.890 --> 02:03.813
So let me take a look at Oldies.

02:05.310 --> 02:07.260
So it has some additional content.

02:07.260 --> 02:11.070
But if we look at git log, it just has the first two commits

02:11.070 --> 02:15.930
from Master and then it has what, three more commits on top?

02:15.930 --> 02:17.970
But nothing has happened to the Master branch

02:17.970 --> 02:22.020
since we split off, since we created this Oldies branch.

02:22.020 --> 02:26.160
So this is an example of what would be a fast forward merge.

02:26.160 --> 02:28.920
Just to be clear here, I'm using this distinction,

02:28.920 --> 02:30.480
I'm calling it a fast forward merge,

02:30.480 --> 02:33.720
but the commands you use are still just git merge

02:33.720 --> 02:35.520
and I'll show you a merge in the next video

02:35.520 --> 02:37.140
that is not a fast forward.

02:37.140 --> 02:38.100
It behaves the same way.

02:38.100 --> 02:39.450
We use the same commands,

02:39.450 --> 02:42.690
you'll just see there's an extra hurdle

02:42.690 --> 02:44.190
along the way potentially.

02:44.190 --> 02:48.240
Anyway, I wanna merge my Oldies branch into Master

02:48.240 --> 02:51.780
so I can get all of this stuff in my Master branch.

02:51.780 --> 02:55.560
So we'll go to Master, git switch master.

02:55.560 --> 02:57.270
That's the first step.

02:57.270 --> 02:59.550
Okay, once again, I do git log.

02:59.550 --> 03:01.560
We only have two commits.

03:01.560 --> 03:03.120
This is where Master leaves off.

03:03.120 --> 03:05.190
This is all that it has.

03:05.190 --> 03:08.070
But now I'm gonna run git merge

03:08.070 --> 03:11.670
and then the name of the branch I want to merge into Master.

03:11.670 --> 03:13.110
I'm currently on Master.

03:13.110 --> 03:15.693
I want to merge the commits from Oldies.

03:16.950 --> 03:18.240
And what do we see?

03:18.240 --> 03:20.340
It says fast forward right there

03:20.340 --> 03:22.050
and it tells me one file change,

03:22.050 --> 03:24.120
seven insertions, one deletion.

03:24.120 --> 03:27.390
Let's take a look at our playlist file now.

03:27.390 --> 03:28.223
Cool.

03:28.223 --> 03:31.110
We can see that all this stuff was added in

03:31.110 --> 03:34.890
and more importantly, if we look at git log,

03:34.890 --> 03:36.750
we see that we had the first commits

03:36.750 --> 03:38.730
that we always had on this branch,

03:38.730 --> 03:41.280
but now we have these other commits that came

03:41.280 --> 03:42.840
from the Oldies branch.

03:42.840 --> 03:45.360
The George Jones commit, George Harrison commit,

03:45.360 --> 03:47.250
and the Van Morrison commit.

03:47.250 --> 03:50.130
And that is our first successful merge.

03:50.130 --> 03:54.150
We merged the content that commits from Oldies on to Master.

03:54.150 --> 03:55.890
Now, a very important note is

03:55.890 --> 03:57.600
that we still have the Oldies branch.

03:57.600 --> 04:00.600
It doesn't delete that branch, it's still there.

04:00.600 --> 04:01.950
So I could go back to Oldies

04:01.950 --> 04:05.850
and do something new and it would not exist on Master.

04:05.850 --> 04:07.620
So they're not forever in sync.

04:07.620 --> 04:10.860
This is one moment where everything from Oldies was added

04:10.860 --> 04:13.830
on to Master, but that doesn't mean that they're the same

04:13.830 --> 04:15.930
and that they'll stay the same if they are.

04:15.930 --> 04:17.370
They're still separate branches

04:17.370 --> 04:18.780
and I can do separate work.

04:18.780 --> 04:22.170
They are distinct contexts, but right now the commits

04:22.170 --> 04:24.420
from Oldies were merged into Master.

04:24.420 --> 04:25.253
And that's an example

04:25.253 --> 04:28.080
of a fast forward merge where the Master branch

04:28.080 --> 04:31.743
in our case had to catch up on commits from a merged branch.
